-- Locations of nodes that a diagnostic points at, across many different location ids.
--
-- The compiler records source locations in one growing array and refers to them by index. Getting
-- that index wrong produces a diagnostic that either points somewhere random or, when the index is
-- out of range, has no location at all - and the indices that used to be computed wrongly were the
-- ones where the array grew, so whether a declaration was reported correctly depended on how many
-- locations the declarations before it happened to record.
--
-- Every statement below is broken in a way whose diagnostic points at a stored node location
-- rather than at the token the parser is on, and there are enough of them, at close enough
-- intervals, to cover the growth boundaries near the start of a file - the ids where such a bug
-- shows up. No line here may report `<no location>`, and every range has to cover the construct
-- named in the message.

{1: 2}
return 1
{-> 1: 2}
{2: 3}
return 2
{-> 2: 3}
{3: 4}
return 3
{-> 3: 4}
{4: 5}
return 4
{-> 4: 5}
{5: 6}
return 5
{-> 5: 6}
{6: 7}
return 6
{-> 6: 7}
{7: 8}
return 7
{-> 7: 8}
{8: 9}
return 8
{-> 8: 9}
{9: 10}
return 9
{-> 9: 10}
{10: 11}
return 10
{-> 10: 11}
